\f1\fs22 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Cracker was still hopeful of accessing the riches Jamison had squirreled away those many years ago, but his knowledge of ancient computer code was minimal. Fucking up the entire system would be the inevitable result of his limited skill set clashing with the old-tech. Sarah was the only one who could help.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He had managed to find an alternate entrance to Jamison Hardy\'92s opulent apartment, avoiding the necessity and hassle of stealing past the heavy security of the exclusive Gravity Well Restaurant. Through an ordeal of trial and error, mostly error, over several weeks of late night jaunts, he blundered across the tycoon\'92s private elevator and now enjoyed easy access from street level. He had all but moved into the apartment, but found himself lonely among the finery. He had never felt lonely in the Pale, there was alway someone screaming or someone stabbing someone for screaming. For that reason he had always coveted solitude, but right now, solitude sucked dick. He wanted to show off his 7-star accommodations, he wanted to be one of those painful new-rich, unable to control ostentatious displays of wealth. 
\f2\i One day soon. 
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Although he was keen to remove every credit from the vast pile Jamison\'92s computers had collected, he had taken on board Sarah\'92s stark warning that messing with the program may have dire consequences to the world economy. \'85 even with a fleeting glimpse of the data-base, she was certain the program controlled a significant chunk of the world debt. It was impossible to predict how, exactly, economies would falter and Sarah was reluctant to tinker and provoke a cascade of losses. In that eventuality the wealthy would, no doubt, throw a tantrum costing thousands, perhaps millions of lives. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He entered the control room but, forbidden to touch anything, was immediately bored. He wandered aimlessly, opening and shutting doors of empty cabinets, pulling out drawers and rifling through the few items left behind. After only a few minutes he had pretty much exhausted all possible sticky-beaking opportunities and had one last door to open. As the final door creaked open, he entered a large, walk in cupboard containing every cleaning product any self respecting commercial cleaner would ever need. There was even an arcane mop and bucket, the utility of which was not lost on Cracker, long desperate to clear the multi-layered dust accumulated by time. He and the mop made their way around the floor with speed and expertise, replacing blackened water and chemical at regular intervals. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He adeptly danced with the mop, gliding across the floor backwards swiping figure 8\'92s, each stroke slicing through the filth and unveiling hardwood of glowing Burgundy. He cleared wide swathes up and down the control room, \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Books! Books made of paper!\'94 The Blessed Mother was shocked. \'93These books must be priceless. Look at them! Beautifully bound and gleaming. This is my idea of heaven.\'94 Drawn to the shelves of neatly stacked hard backs of every kind, she opened books at random and nose-dived into the pages to deeply draw in the scent of printers ink and bleached paper. \'93They\'92ve never been opened! What a tragedy. No one have ever read them?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Take what you like.\'94 Cracker kindly offered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You don\'92t actually own any of this, not yet anyway.\'94 Sarah warned. \'93At the moment you are merely the most successful squatter in history.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay, I will keep them myself.\'94 He did not know what he would use them for. Reading them certainly wasn\'92t high on the agenda. 
\f2\i Door stop, perhaps? Fat Frisbee?\

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Cracker was glad to see Sarah, but was surprised to see Alex by their side but motioned them into the lounge area of the vast apartment. \'93What brings you here?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I need access to the finance data base and I need Alex to be there.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He knows about it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, I need him to contact his mother at precisely the right moment.\'94 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Great. Did you tell anyone else?\'94 Cracker replied.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No. Trust me on this one, I need to cement her as an ally.\'94 Sarah face was blank, but Richard grasped his own hands with fingers interlocked and silently pleaded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s not as if I can say no, certainly not to Richard.\'94 Cracker smiled at a laughing Richard who unclasped his hand clapped and kicked his legs wildly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay, let\'92s do this thing.\'94 Sarah said as she left the room and paced with purpose toward the entry to the control room, Richard giggling as he bounced on her hip. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93After you, Alex.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This is a really lovely apartment. What area are we in?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You don\'92t know you where you are?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I never take any notice, you know me.\'94 Alex turned and followed after Sarah. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah was seated at the main console for the control centre and Cracker set to swiping the inside of his cheek with his finger and smearing the ID panel. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Blood would have been quicker.\'94 Sarah noted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No thanks.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After a few minutes the monitors booted up and final security details scrolled up the screen, listing everything identifiable in his saliva. After a long list of bacteria, fruit, various meat-stuffs and narcotics filled the screen, the name Hardy, Jamison 51% + 49% unknown.\'94 Security must have calculated this was close enough and they were granted access. Sarah took the reigns and got to work, stopped once only when she reached some sort of an impasse. Richard grasped a good chunk of her hair and pulled with all his might, stealing her attention away from the screen that refused to give her the answer she wanted. A short glance in his eyes and a bright smile appeared across her face. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of course, you little genius!\'94 Sarah kissed gently on his forehead. \'93It\'92s really quite simple when you look at it that way.\'94 Richard giggled as Sarah worked on the old machine. \'93Okay, I think we are ready. Alex call your mother.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Mother? It\'92s me, Alex.\'94 Shakti stuttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What do you want?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I am with Sarah in a giant computer room, but it\'92s really old. There\'92s old keyboards and only 2D screens and and Sarah says it\'92s Jamison Hardy\'92s old central control room.\'94 Alex waited for a reply. \'93Hello? Mother?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Give hand the device to Sarah, dear.\'94 Eloise mind was spinning. 
\f2\i It\'92s been found!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hello, Eloise.\'94 Sarah smiled down the phone. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah dear. Listen to me carefully. If you are where you claim to be you will need help gaining access. Security will be tight.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We are already in.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You don\'92t know what you are dealing with, very powerful forces. If you interfere with the natural order, economies would be devastated\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If you have any money in off-shore accounts, investments in finance organisations that cater only to the super-wealthy?\'94 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I have heard of these places.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, the tax havens. If you do what I say, tomorrow you be considerable more wealthy than you are now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What do you want me to do?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It might be a good idea to divest yourself of any off-shore accounts or investments immediately. I am realiably informed about one third of that investment money will become inaccessible for a period of 24 hours.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What area of investment will be safe during this period.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I would strongly urge you to invest in Elvis Corporation stock.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You are kidding.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I want you and a all of your richest friends to take out all of your money, all at the same time. If the withdrawals are of sufficient size, you will sett off alarm bells all over the world. I will shut off the flow of money and when others of your ilk try to withdrawal their money, they will get nothing. I expect a run on these banks and huge losses and by the time the tap is turned some will already be closed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why Elvis?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93For what I have in mind, Elvis stocks will be safe. You will get get make your friends rich and enemies poor, depending on who you choose to tell. We will send a message to the most powerful. That we can get them where it hurts.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93For what purpose?\'94 Eloise said, horrified by the audacity.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It is just a demonstration. Most investors will only be moderately inconvenienced. Some of course, those already on the edge, may not survive even one day without liquidity. The CEO must be replaced or further demonstrations will be made. I may not be so charatible as to give you forewarning next time. You and your friend\'92s will make sure he goes down. Richard is in mortal danger if our current CEO stays in place.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93When? Will this happen?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We are ready right now. Make your calls you have one hour. The people you save will owe you for life.\'94 The clock had started ticking. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We will not want stock, just our money and a reasonable return on the investment, say 5%?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93For one day? 1%\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I thought you might fall for that one. 2%\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93At 100% per annum it is less a than 0.3% for a day. How about 1.5%?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You drive a hard bargain, but I am feeling charitable. No shares, just a loan 1.5% for one day. How much can you handle?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Whatever you got.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Immediate response to the massive transfer of funds from some of the biggest players on the planet did not go unnoticed. Obviously there was something amiss with the upper-tier banking system and everyone wanted out. Thousands of withdrawals were denied. The banks could not meeting the demand, their leverage ratios were way too high and they many ceased trading for the day. The stock market, always looking for a good reason to plummet vertical, panicked and shrank like an erection room with a vomiting fat chick.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A large chuck of the backroom cash had stopped moving and no amount of manipulating the system seemed to clear the log-jam. It could not just disappear, but that did not stop half the world grinding to a halt when money slowed to a trickle. No-one knew where the money lay. Somewhere in the system most would surmise, hoping it would reset and all would be well, but it was impossible to know when it may happen and they all had to assume they had just driven off a cliff. Bridging finance was immediately scarce, except for the loans offered from the good people at Elvis Corporation Savings and Loans. They seemed to have plenty of money to throw around. Using the temporarily quarantined cash, Elvis Corporation Saving and Loans were effectively loaning desperate investors their own money at outrageous interest rates. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Although the loan scam was lucrative, it was miniscule in comparison to the money Sarah began to throw around. She diverted the balance of the funds into millions of short burst high risk micro-trades, programing the old system to preemptively act, knowing the conditions were temporary and predicting the market a millisecond at a time. Some bets went poorly, but the vast majority were yielded high returns. The amount of money invested in this manner at any given moment could buy a reasonable sized nation-state and the yield, Sarah calculated, would more than cover the loans doled out by Elvis Corp savings and loan. When Sarah turned the tap back on and her ingenuous algorithm had covered it\'92s tracks, many of the world\'92s richest people were left with on few lousy million and the Elvis Corporation controlled a decent chunk of the world\'92s debt. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well dear that worked out well for all of us. I will need my investment back immediately plus my 1.5% interest. I see Elvis Corp has up 500% in one day, I guess I should have bought stock. Property prices have plummeted and I am one of the few with money.\'94 Eloise told Sarah when she called.. \'93You must come around to my house some time soon. We have much to discuss. 
\f2\i No-one should have that much power. Except me.
